<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bergen auf Rügen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> auf Rügen is the main inland town of the island of Rügen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecklenburg-vorpommern">Mecklenburg-Vorpommern</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Its Church of St Mary was begun in the late 12th century by Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaromar-i-of-rugen">Jaromar I of Rügen</a> and given in 1193 to a convent of Cistercian nuns, around which the town grew. It long served as the administrative centre of the island.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bergen-auf-rugen.jpg" alt="Bergen auf Rügen" width="320" height="258" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hagar66 based on work of TUBS · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bergen_auf_R%C3%BCgen_in_VR.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> auf Rügen lies in the middle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s largest island, Rügen, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> coast. The town is built on a glacial moraine left when the ice sheets retreated at the end of the last ice age, in hilly country. The Rugard woods rise to 91 metres on its north-eastern edge, and the surrounding land is mostly farmland. Two shallow bays lie near it: the Kleiner Jasmunder Bodden to the north-east and the Greifswalder Bodden to the south-east.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The island was settled in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> by the Rani, a Slavic people who kept a pagan worship site at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkona">Cape Arkona</a>, defended by a fort, the Jaromarsburg. A castle stood on the Rugard hill in the area of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> subdued the Rani they set up the Principality of Rügen under a local dynasty, and the Rugard became an administrative centre. The principality became Christian and received German settlers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostsiedlung">Ostsiedlung</a>. The town itself was built on the neighbouring hilltop, outside the ramparts of the Rugard. Its name comes from the Slavic word gora, mountain, and is recorded in 1232 as the place Gora.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkona">Jaromarsburg</a> fell in 1168, and soon afterwards work began on St Mary's as the palace church of Prince Jaromar I. In 1193 it became a Cistercian convent, and the foundation encouraged the first inn, built in 1232. In 1325 the principality passed by inheritance to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>. Fires, such as that of 1445, nearly destroyed the town, the convent and part of the church. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> reached Pomerania in 1534, and the dukes took over the convent. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> law town rights in 1613, passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1648 as part of Swedish Pomerania, and became part of the Prussian Province of Pomerania in 1815.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> auf Rügen is in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecklenburg-vorpommern">Mecklenburg-Western Pomerania</a> and is the administrative seat of its Amt, which has more than 23,000 inhabitants. Railways reached it in 1883. St Mary's, the former convent church, is the oldest surviving brick building in Mecklenburg-Western Pomerania and has the only complete painting of a church interior in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The town museum occupies a restored abbey building and covers the history of Rügen from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-age">Stone Age</a>. The Ernst Moritz Arndt tower, completed in 1877, gives views over much of the island.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bergen auf Rügen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They founded monasteries such as <b>Bergen</b> and Eldena and invited German settlers, so Low German gradually replaced the Slavic speech of the Rani.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-rugen">Principality of Rügen</a></li></ul></section>
